description:
In no_ack.rs lines 102-111, FCN assignment handles wrap-around when fragments exceed FCN range:

```rust
let fcn = if is_last {
    fcn_max::<N>() // All-1
} else {
    // Count down from max-1
    let max_fcn = fcn_max::<N>() - 1;
    let offset = index as u8;
    if offset > max_fcn {
        // Wrap around if more fragments than FCN range
        max_fcn - (offset % (max_fcn + 1))
    } else {
        max_fcn - offset
    }
};
```

What goes wrong: The modular arithmetic `offset % (max_fcn + 1)` is non-obvious. Why wrap? What does it mean for reassembly? Does the receiver expect this?

The comment "Wrap around if more fragments than FCN range" doesn't explain the implication: No-Ack mode doesn't have windows, so when fragments exceed 2^N-1, FCN values repeat. This is valid per RFC 8724 for No-Ack because reassembly relies on arrival order, not FCN uniqueness.

Suggested comment:
```rust
// No-Ack mode has no windows. When fragment count exceeds (2^N - 1),
// FCN values wrap. This is safe because No-Ack reassembly is order-based;
// the receiver appends tiles in arrival order, ignoring FCN for placement.
// FCN only distinguishes All-1 (final fragment with RCS) from regular tiles.
```

